I fixed the leak and committed it as f0e0869f. The message carries Fixes #1022. fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the full test suite all pass.

What was wrong: the fence only checked the folder you pass to search and list_files. The tool then searched everything underneath it. So search CANARY . under --deny-dir secrets was allowed, because . is allowed, and it showed the model ./secrets/key.txt:1:token=CANARY.

What changed:
• **New `src/fence_result_filter.rs:** filters the output of search and list_files` only.
  • A list_files line is removed if that path is refused.
  • A search line is removed if the path in front of any :<digits>: is refused. It tries every such split, not just the first, so a file or folder name that itself contains :12: gets withheld rather than leaked.
  • Removed lines are replaced by one plain-text line giving the count. If nothing is removed, the output is exactly the same as before; a test pins that.
• **src/tool_wrappers.rs:** both wrappers, the main agent's and the sub-agents', now run the filter on what the tool returns. They use the same refusal check as the folder argument, which already includes the /cd-added deny list and resolves symlinks, so there is no second rule to keep in sync.
• **docs/src/configuration/permissions.md:** one sentence saying search and list results inside a refused folder are withheld, with a note.

Two changes from the task spec:
• **Note wording:** it reads "…a directory the fence refuses (--deny-dir / --allow-dir / [directories])" instead of the spec's deny-only text. The refusal check also enforces --allow-dir, so the deny-only wording would sometimes be wrong.
• **An existing test changed:** `cd_config_note`'s source check counts calls to the `/cd`-aware refusal in `tool_wrappers.rs` and expected exactly 2. It now expects exactly 4, with a comment explaining the two new calls are the result filters.

Verification:
• Tests cover the canary file, an allowed file next to it surviving unchanged, the no-change case, line parsing (including "b:12:c" in the matched text and non-ASCII paths), and the :N:-in-a-name case.
• One test runs end to end through the real wrappers and the real yoagent SearchTool and ListFilesTool on a temp folder, covering both the main-agent and sub-agent wrappers.
• To prove the tests can fail, I disabled the filter in a single command that also restored it. The 5 tests that expect lines withheld failed by name, including the end-to-end one, and they passed again after the restore.
• I did **not** run the live yoyo -p probe. The end-to-end test stands in for it.

Left on #1022 (named in the commit):
• rg still runs without --no-config, so a RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH file can still pass flags like --pre=cmd to it.
• The tool's own (N matches) / (N files) line and its details JSON still count withheld results; the note gives the true count.
• find warning lines are kept as-is even if they name a denied path.

I committed only my own files. .yoyo/risk_weights.json was already modified before I started and is still uncommitted.
